-- Simplifies the DJI UAV project page (general text, more images) and moves the full technical detail,
-- tables and figures to the publication page. Figures are uploaded to storage under projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/.
-- Supersedes the project rewrite in 20261015_update_dji_uav_project_from_paper.sql.

update public.projects set
  subtitle = 'A lightweight drone-mounted 3D mapping system that works without GPS, using the drone''s own built-in sensor.',
  summary = 'A mapping payload for the DJI Matrice M350 RTK drone that builds accurate 3D maps of outdoor areas without relying on GPS. A LiDAR scanner and a small onboard computer reuse the drone''s built-in motion sensor, so no extra sensor is needed.',
  description = $d$This project is a lightweight 3D mapping payload for the DJI Matrice M350 RTK drone. It lets the drone build an accurate map of an outdoor area without relying on GPS, which matters around tall buildings where satellite signals become unreliable.

A LiDAR scanner and a small NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX computer ride on the drone. Instead of adding another motion sensor, the system reuses the drone's own built-in IMU, which keeps the payload cheaper, lighter and simpler to build. The whole mapping process runs onboard using ROS 2.$d$,
  tags = array['UAV','RKO-LIO','LiDAR-Inertial Odometry','ROS 2','Sensor Fusion','GPS-denied Navigation','DJI E-Port','Edge Computing'],
  tools = array['DJI Matrice M350 RTK','NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX','Velodyne VLP-16','ROS 2 Humble','DJI Payload SDK','RKO-LIO','UART','USB Bulk','DC-DC Power'],
  outcomes = array['Accurate 3D map of a campus area, built entirely onboard the drone','No external IMU needed: lower cost, weight and build complexity','Runs comfortably on a small Jetson Orin NX computer','Checked against real building measurements, with about 1.6% average error'],
  publication_url = 'https://mohammedbajhaw.github.io/publications/2',
  rich_content = $r$<h2>What the project is about</h2><p>Drones often rely on GPS to know where they are, but near large buildings the signal degrades or disappears. This project gives a drone another way to understand its surroundings: it scans the environment with a LiDAR and builds a 3D map while it flies, with no help from satellites.</p><figure><img src="https://engportfolio-zhkmdjuy.manus.space/manus-storage/figure-005_82c208cf.jpg" alt="Comparison of the surveyed outdoor area and reconstructed coloured point cloud" /><figcaption>The surveyed outdoor area next to the coloured 3D map the drone reconstructed.</figcaption></figure><h2>The payload</h2><p>The payload is built around a Velodyne VLP-16 LiDAR and an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX computer, mounted on a DJI Matrice M350 RTK. Power and communication circuits were designed so the whole unit runs from the drone and talks to it over its E-Port.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/drone-payload-in-flight.png" alt="The mapping payload mounted on the DJI Matrice M350 RTK during a night flight" /><figcaption>The complete mapping payload mounted on the drone during a test flight.</figcaption></figure><h2>How it works</h2><ul><li><strong>Sensing:</strong> the LiDAR scans the surroundings while the drone's built-in IMU reports how it is moving.</li><li><strong>Computing:</strong> the Jetson Orin NX combines both streams with the RKO-LIO method to track the drone and grow the 3D map in real time.</li><li><strong>Simplicity:</strong> because the drone's own IMU is used, there is no extra sensor to buy, mount or calibrate.</li></ul><h2>Flying and mapping</h2><p>The system was flown around a university building with trees, parked cars and complex facades. The drone climbed, circled and landed while the map was being built onboard.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/onboard-trajectory.png" alt="3D flight path estimated onboard, coloured by altitude" /><figcaption>The flight path estimated onboard, coloured by altitude.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/satellite-vs-point-cloud.png" alt="Satellite view of the university buildings next to the reconstructed 3D point cloud" /><figcaption>Satellite view of the site (left) next to the 3D map built from the flight (right).</figcaption></figure><h2>Checking the map</h2><p>To see whether the map can be trusted, several real building walls were measured on site with laser rangefinders and compared with the same distances in the 3D map. They agreed closely, which shows the map keeps the true proportions of the buildings.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/facade-segments.png" alt="Building facade segments used to compare real measurements with the 3D map" /><figcaption>The building walls that were measured on site and compared with the map.</figcaption></figure><h2>What the work covers</h2><ul><li>Hardware integration of the drone, computer and LiDAR, including power and communication circuits.</li><li>The ROS 2 software stack that connects the sensors to the mapping method.</li><li>A field flight test and evaluation of how the mapping method behaves on a drone.</li></ul><p>The measurements, tables and extra figures from this work are in the research paper, summarised on the <a href="https://mohammedbajhaw.github.io/publications/2">publication page</a>.</p>$r$,
  updated_at = now()
where slug = 'rko-lio-dji-uav-gps-denied-mapping';

insert into public.project_media (project_id, storage_path, alt, sort_order)
select p.id, v.storage_path, v.alt, v.sort_order from public.projects p cross join (values
  ('projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/drone-payload-in-flight.png', 'Mapping payload mounted on the DJI Matrice M350 RTK during a test flight', 1),
  ('projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/satellite-vs-point-cloud.png', 'Satellite view of the university buildings next to the reconstructed 3D point cloud', 2),
  ('projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/onboard-trajectory.png', '3D flight path estimated onboard, coloured by altitude', 3),
  ('projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/facade-segments.png', 'Building walls measured on site to check the 3D map', 4)
) as v(storage_path, alt, sort_order)
where p.slug = 'rko-lio-dji-uav-gps-denied-mapping' and not exists (select 1 from public.project_media m where m.project_id = p.id and m.storage_path = v.storage_path);

update public.publications set
  authors = 'Mohammed Ali Mohammed S. Bajhaw; Anas Mahyoub Naji Saeed Alqadhi; Munef el Muhammed; Aysegul Ucar',
  summary = 'A hardware and software framework that integrates a DJI Matrice M350 RTK with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX over ROS 2, using only a Velodyne VLP-16 and the drone''s built-in 200 Hz IMU for GPS-denied LiDAR-inertial mapping with RKO-LIO.',
  details = $p$<h2>Abstract</h2><p>This work presents a hardware and software integration framework for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) operating in GPS-denied environments. We detail the integration of a DJI Matrice M350 RTK drone with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX edge computing platform using Robot Operating System (ROS 2). To minimize payload cost, weight, and mechanical complexity, the system relies exclusively on a Velodyne VLP-16 LiDAR whose data is fused with telemetry from the drone's built-in high-precision Inertial Measurement Unit (IMU) nominally streaming at 200 Hz, removing the need for expensive external IMUs. We deploy RKO-LIO, a recent scan-to-map method that replaces sensor-specific noise modeling with a simplified IMU motion model and an adaptive gravity-orientation regularizer.</p><p>From a 458.49-meter outdoor flight reaching 38.28 m altitude we report three findings. First, the map produced onboard during flight reached an average structural error of 1.57% against laser-rangefinder measurements of six facade segments, even though latency spikes caused 41.3% of the LiDAR scans to be skipped. Second, the method's default 0.5 m association threshold is conservative for aerial operation: the inter-beam spacing of a 16-beam LiDAR exceeds it beyond 14 m range, where 52.8% of our returns lie, and the registration rejects 2.0% of point correspondences at 0.5 m against 0.1% at 5.0 m. Relaxing it reduces the final landing-point position error, validated against a physical ground measurement, from 1.58 m to 0.31 m (0.343% to 0.067% of path length). Third, we characterize the timestamp fidelity of the DJI built-in IMU, delivered in bursts with 12.2% of samples arriving after gaps exceeding 15 ms.</p><h2>1. Introduction</h2><p>UAVs are used for search and rescue, industrial inspection and 3D mapping, and most of these applications rely heavily on GPS. Near urban canyons and buildings GPS signals degrade or vanish, so alternative sensing is needed. LiDAR is a powerful tool for building accurate 3D maps thanks to its illumination invariance and precise ranging, but on its own it suffers from motion skew distortion and poor performance in feature-poor environments.</p><p>LiDAR is therefore fused with an IMU in LiDAR-inertial odometry (LIO). Popular algorithms such as LOAM, LIO-SAM and FAST-LIO2 are highly successful but accumulate drift among dynamic objects, and they often need expensive high-frequency external IMUs such as the Pixhawk Cube Orange to stay stable.</p><p>The paper makes three contributions:</p><ol><li>A robust, cost-effective integration of a DJI M350 RTK with an NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX that relies entirely on the drone's internal IMU, nominally 200 Hz, read through the DJI Payload SDK, removing the cost, weight and calibration burden of a dedicated inertial unit.</li><li>A quantitative characterization of the telemetry path itself: the built-in IMU reaches ROS 2 in bursts rather than at a uniform 200 Hz, with 12.2% of samples arriving after gaps exceeding 15 ms, three times the nominal period.</li><li>A practical evaluation of RKO-LIO on an aerial platform, where the onboard map reached 1.57% average facade-measurement error and the default association threshold proved conservative for a 16-beam LiDAR at altitude. Relaxing it lowers the final landing-point error from 1.58 m to 0.31 m over a 458.49 m flight.</li></ol><p>This study differs from the earlier indoor work in platform, environment and inertial source: that work used an external Pixhawk IMU indoors, whereas this system uses no external IMU and operates outdoors at up to 38.28 m altitude.</p><h2>2. Hardware integration and architecture</h2><p>Real-time remote-controlled mapping needs high computational power within the strict weight, power and cost limits of a UAV.</p><h3>UAV platform</h3><p>The DJI Matrice M350 RTK was chosen for its reliability in industrial applications, a payload capacity of up to 2.7 kg and IP55 weather resistance. Its E-Port interface allows direct integration of custom payloads, providing both power and data transfer.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/drone-payload-in-flight.png" alt="The complete 3D mapping payload mounted on the DJI Matrice M350 RTK" /><figcaption>Fig. 1. The complete 3D mapping payload mounted on the DJI Matrice M350 RTK UAV.</figcaption></figure><h3>Edge computing platform</h3><p>An NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX module (16 GB RAM) runs the LiDAR processing and the ROS 2 SLAM algorithms. It balances power consumption and performance, up to 100 TOPS, and has built-in support for the JetPack SDK, CUDA and TensorRT.</p><h3>LiDAR sensor</h3><p>A Velodyne VLP-16 provides 360-degree coverage and a precise point cloud at 10 Hz. It connects to the Jetson over Ethernet and is powered through a dedicated voltage step-down circuit.</p><h3>Built-in IMU integration</h3><p>Instead of adding an external high-frequency IMU such as a MicroStrain or Pixhawk Cube Orange, which increases calibration complexity, hardware cost and payload weight, a mechanism was devised to extract data from the high-precision IMU inside the DJI M350. The inertial data travels from the flight controller to the Jetson over the native telemetry link, so no clock synchronization between an external IMU and the flight state is needed. The interface advertises 200 Hz, but the timestamp analysis in Section 5.4 shows samples actually arrive in bursts rather than at a uniform 5 ms interval. This must be accounted for during inertial integration and scan deskewing, while still removing the need for an external IMU.</p><h3>Communication architecture</h3><p>The USB Bulk protocol carries high-bandwidth data, connecting the E-Port output to the Jetson's USB-A port. UART is the low-latency link for direct drone control, receiving the IMU data and extracting the instantaneous take-off coordinates at 230400 bps. This lets the system acquire the built-in IMU data at its nominal 200 Hz rate, giving the dense motion constraints LIO needs without external sensors.</p><p><strong>Table I. Computational resource profiling on the NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX</strong></p><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Computing platform</td><td>NVIDIA Jetson Orin NX (16 GB)</td></tr><tr><td>OS / middleware</td><td>Ubuntu 22.04 / ROS 2 Humble</td></tr><tr><td>LiDAR processing frequency</td><td>10 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Nominal IMU data rate</td><td>200 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>Average CPU utilization</td><td>25.79% (peak 31.9%)</td></tr><tr><td>Average RAM usage</td><td>about 1.99 GB / 1,997.8 MB (peak 2.01 GB / 2,011.7 MB)</td></tr><tr><td>Core operating temperatures</td><td>CPU average 56.47 °C / GPU average 53.94 °C</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>3. Software framework and middleware</h2><p>A ROS 2 (Humble) architecture provides inter-process communication and synchronization between the physical components. A strict Transform (TF) tree was defined for the mapping algorithms: <code>base_link</code> is the drone's center of mass, where the built-in IMU is located, and a rigid transformation to the LiDAR frame (<code>velodyne</code>) was defined from physical millimeter measurements of the mounting location. This static description lets vibration and deviation sensed by the built-in IMU be compensated mathematically on the LiDAR point cloud during odometry.</p><h2>4. LiDAR-inertial odometry without sensor-specific modeling</h2><h3>Limitations of traditional SLAM algorithms</h3><p>Frameworks such as LOAM and LIO-SAM extract edge and planar features and match them to correct drift. Real environments produce noise from dynamic objects such as walking people, swaying trees and moving vehicles, and from distorted features such as shiny metal pipes that reflect laser beams specularly. Traditional algorithms often treat these erroneous points as static features, leading to accumulated drift, ghosting artifacts and map distortion.</p><h3>RKO-LIO and its adaptive regularization</h3><p>RKO-LIO replaces sensor-specific noise modeling with a physics-based kinematic motion model and an adaptive gravity-orientation regularization. It avoids both the iterated Kalman filtering of FAST-LIO2 and the pose-graph optimization of LIO-SAM, which is what lets it operate without a characterized IMU noise model. Accelerometer and gyroscope samples between two consecutive LiDAR frames are averaged and integrated with a constant-acceleration model, and the resulting relative pose both deskews the scan and initializes the registration.</p><p>Registration minimizes the unweighted mean squared point-to-point residual against a VDB voxel map, following the point-to-point scheme of KISS-ICP. Outliers, including returns from moving vehicles and swaying foliage, are rejected by a hard association gate: a point contributes to the optimization only if its nearest map neighbor lies within a fixed distance, and is discarded otherwise. There is no continuous down-weighting. The novel component is an adaptive gravity-orientation term weighted by 1/β, where β = β₀(1 + σa²) and σa is the standard deviation of the accelerometer magnitude over the frame interval. When the platform vibrates or accelerates aggressively, σa grows and the gravity prior is automatically de-weighted in favor of the LiDAR measurement.</p><p>Fig. 3 shows why the fixed distance matters for a 16-beam LiDAR such as the VLP-16, whose adjacent beams are 2° apart vertically. On a surface at range R they land R·tan(2°) apart, which exceeds the 0.5 m default gate beyond R = 14.3 m, so the gate becomes restrictive for farther returns. 52.8% of the returns in this flight lie beyond that range. A point rejected by the gate is left out of the pose correction only, while the full scan is still inserted into the local map.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/association-gate-geometry.png" alt="Association-gate geometry for a 16-beam LiDAR" /><figcaption>Fig. 3. Association-gate geometry for a 16-beam LiDAR: spacing of adjacent beams versus the 0.5 m default gate, and share of the flight's returns per range interval.</figcaption></figure><h2>5. Field experiments and validation</h2><h3>5.1 Dynamic outdoor flight and facade trajectory analysis</h3><p>The payload was flown on the DJI Matrice M350 RTK in a mixed outdoor environment around the university campus buildings, with swaying tree branches, complex facade geometries and active vehicles in the parking lot. The trajectory covered 458.49 m over 484.21 s (8 minutes 4 seconds). The drone executed 6-DOF maneuvers and recorded 4,802 LiDAR scans at an average speed of 0.95 m/s and a peak velocity of 3.95 m/s. The offline reconstruction yields one pose per scan, whereas the onboard run produced 2,819 poses and ended 29.15 s before touchdown. The onboard map is used for the facade validation, and the complete reconstruction for the parameter study.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/satellite-vs-point-cloud.png" alt="Aerial view of the university buildings compared with the RKO-LIO reconstructed point cloud" /><figcaption>Fig. 4. Google Earth satellite view of the university buildings (left) next to the RKO-LIO reconstructed 3D global point cloud, coloured by height (right).</figcaption></figure><p>The main building, its neighbors and the rows of trees are recognizable in both views, while the roof interior is empty because it was barely scanned.</p><p>For the onboard trajectory, the spatial coverage was a 2D bounding-box area of 5,673.91 m² and a 3D bounding-box volume of 190,343 m³. The onboard run reached a maximum altitude of 33.55 m above the take-off point (38.28 m in the complete reconstruction) with a maximum vertical speed of 3.92 m/s. Flight kinematics showed an average acceleration of 1.23 m/s², a peak acceleration of 6.46 m/s² (gravity-compensated, taken directly from the IMU) and a velocity standard deviation of 0.63 m/s, so the platform was exercised over a wide range of dynamics.</p><p>Fig. 5 shows the trajectory estimated onboard (2,819 poses over 455.1 s): a loop around the building, about 100 m in X and 58 m in Y, mostly between 0.5 and 14 m altitude, with two steep climbs to 32.2 m and 33.5 m. The green marker is the take-off point and the red marker, at (9.79, −0.75, 3.69) m, is the last onboard pose, reached 29.15 s before touchdown.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/onboard-trajectory.png" alt="3D trajectory estimated onboard, coloured by altitude" /><figcaption>Fig. 5. 3D trajectory estimated onboard (2,819 poses), coloured by altitude. Green: take-off point; red: last onboard pose.</figcaption></figure><h3>5.2 Facade measurement validation</h3><p>Six outer building facade segments (A to F) were measured with high-precision laser rangefinders to establish physical ground-truth distances and compared with the corresponding distances extracted from the reconstructed point cloud. Segments A, D and E run along the lower part of the facade, B across its upper part, C along a vertical edge, and F on the ground in a top-down view.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/facade-segments.png" alt="Building facade measurement segments A to F annotated on photographs" /><figcaption>Fig. 6. Building facade measurement segments A to F annotated on physical photographs of the university buildings.</figcaption></figure><p><strong>Table II. Comparison of physical ground truth and RKO-LIO distance estimations</strong></p><table><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Real-world distance (m)</th><th>RKO-LIO map distance (m)</th><th>Absolute error (m)</th><th>Relative error (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>38.87</td><td>38.50</td><td>0.37</td><td>0.95%</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>26.21</td><td>26.50</td><td>0.29</td><td>1.11%</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>27.59</td><td>28.10</td><td>0.51</td><td>1.85%</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>36.41</td><td>37.31</td><td>0.90</td><td>2.47%</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>39.91</td><td>40.35</td><td>0.44</td><td>1.10%</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>24.10</td><td>24.57</td><td>0.47</td><td>1.95%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Absolute errors for segments B to F range between 0.29 m and 0.90 m, relative errors below 2.5%. Segment A, the longest span at 38.87 m, registered a 0.37 m absolute error, a 0.95% relative error. Across all six segments the average relative structural error is 1.57% and the maximum 2.47%.</p><h3>5.3 Computational resource profiling on edge hardware</h3><p>The whole software stack, including the psdk_ros2 node, LiDAR pre-processing and the RKO-LIO optimization, was profiled on the Jetson Orin NX during the 458.49 m flight. The mapping pipeline consumed an average of 25.79% CPU (peak 31.9%) and 1,997.81 MB of RAM (about 1.99 GB, 12.19% of the 16 GB total; peak 2,011.7 MB, 12.3%). Under active flight dynamics and a 15 W operational profile, CPU and GPU temperatures stabilized at averages of 56.47 °C (peak 57.3 °C) and 53.94 °C (peak 54.3 °C), ensuring thermal safety (Fig. 7).</p><p>The CPU figure is averaged over the eight cores and was recorded while a full-rate sensor bag (about 18 MB/s) was being written concurrently, so deployment load is strictly lower. It should not be read as real-time margin (see Section 5.5).</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/resource-profile.png" alt="Onboard CPU, RAM and temperature profile on the Jetson Orin NX" /><figcaption>Fig. 7. Onboard hardware resource utilization on the Jetson Orin NX showing CPU, RAM and temperature during the final portion of the mission and the subsequent sequential component shutdown.</figcaption></figure><h3>5.4 Parameter sensitivity and validation against ground truth</h3><p>The landing point was measured on site and expressed in the take-off frame as (X, Y, Z) = (8.46, −1.20, −0.16) m. Because the trajectory is estimated without any satellite or motion-capture reference, this single point is the only external datum available, so the final position error is reported rather than an absolute trajectory error. Table III sweeps the two parameters governing registration, the association gate and the regularization weight β₀, with everything else, including the input data, held identical.</p><p><strong>Table III. Final position error against the measured landing point</strong> (DD: optional double-downsampling step)</p><table><thead><tr><th>Gate (m)</th><th>DD</th><th>3-D error (m)</th><th>% of path</th><th>Rejected (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.25</td><td>on</td><td>3.489</td><td>0.710</td><td>14.5</td></tr><tr><td>0.50 (default)</td><td>on</td><td>1.583</td><td>0.343</td><td>2.0</td></tr><tr><td>0.50</td><td>off</td><td>1.886</td><td>0.410</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>1.00</td><td>on</td><td>0.489</td><td>0.107</td><td>0.4</td></tr><tr><td>5.00</td><td>on</td><td>0.307</td><td>0.067</td><td>0.1</td></tr><tr><td>5.00</td><td>off</td><td>0.343</td><td>0.075</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>β₀ = 0</td><td>on</td><td>1.082</td><td>0.236</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>β₀ = 1000</td><td>on</td><td>1.256</td><td>0.272</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Relaxing the gate from the 0.5 m default to 1.0 m or beyond lowers the error by roughly a factor of five. The share of point correspondences rejected by the gate falls from 14.5% at 0.25 m to 2.0% at 0.5 m, 0.4% at 1.0 m and 0.1% at 5.0 m, in step with the error. This follows from the roughly 2° vertical resolution of a 16-beam scanner and therefore depends on flight altitude and point density. It is not a claim that a 0.5 m gate is inappropriate for ground-level platforms, denser scanners or other registration schemes.</p><figure><img src="https://ntynaiuxqylscazugcdo.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/portfolio-media/projects/rko-lio-dji-uav/altitude-by-gate.png" alt="Estimated altitude for the parameter settings, whole flight and last 40 seconds" /><figcaption>Fig. 8. Estimated altitude for the settings of Table III: (a) whole flight; (b) last 40 s, with the measured landing height (dotted line).</figcaption></figure><p>Up to about 350 s all curves stay within about 2.2 m of each other at altitudes up to 38 m. From about 400 s the 0.25 m gate separates and ends 3.3 m above the take-off altitude. In the last 40 s, the 5.0 m gate ends closest to the measured landing height of −0.16 m (−0.11 m), then the 1.0 m gate (−0.56 m), while the default gate ends 1.5 m below it (−1.70 m).</p><p>Given a ground-truth uncertainty of about ±0.15 m, the three best rows are not separable from one another, but their ordering relative to the default is unambiguous. The systematic +0.23 m offset in the Y axis common to every row corresponds to a 1.56° error in the assumed take-off heading and is a property of the measurement frame, not of the odometry. The rotation-invariant horizontal magnitude is 8.545 m measured against 8.401 m estimated.</p><h3>5.5 Real-time behavior and frame loss</h3><p>The onboard pipeline processed 2,819 of the 4,802 LiDAR scans, so 41.3% were skipped. The mean cost per scan was 80.5 ms, within the 100 ms sensor period, but latency spikes (95th percentile 179.8 ms) combined with a subscriber queue one element deep caused the next arriving scan to be discarded. Nevertheless, the onboard map reached 1.57% average structural error, so the skipped scans did not prevent accurate mapping in this flight.</p><p>The inertial stream was examined as well. The DJI telemetry is advertised at 200 Hz and its mean inter-sample interval of 5.01 ms agrees with that figure, but the 96,669 samples arrive in bursts: 66.2% of intervals are shorter than 3 ms (median 2.35 ms), while 12.2% of samples follow a gap exceeding 15 ms, three times the nominal period, the longest being 41.3 ms. This is a property of the PSDK transport path rather than of the inertial sensor, and any odometry method that assumes uniformly sampled inertial input should take it into account.</p><h2>6. Conclusion and future work</h2><p>This paper presented a cost-effective UAV mapping payload and a field validation of RKO-LIO driven by the drone's built-in IMU. On the hardware side, relying entirely on the built-in IMU via the DJI E-Port and designing efficient UART and DC-DC power circuits removes the need for an additional external IMU and simplifies payload integration. On the software side, results across a dynamic 458.49 m outdoor flight characterized the behavior of RKO-LIO on an aerial platform. Its default 0.5 m association gate proved conservative for a 16-beam LiDAR at altitude; relaxing it reduced the final landing-point error from 1.58 m to 0.31 m, i.e. 0.067% of the trajectory. Structural accuracy on the facade segments stayed at an average relative error of 1.57% (maximum 2.47%). Post-flight analysis also showed that latency spikes made the onboard run skip 41.3% of the LiDAR scans without affecting the primary objective, and the payload stayed within a modest resource envelope of 25.79% CPU and 1.99 GB RAM.</p><p><strong>Limitations.</strong> The results come from a single flight, and no continuous reference trajectory was available (the aircraft RTK solution was not logged and the site had no motion-capture coverage). Absolute trajectory error and relative pose error therefore cannot be computed; the final position error is reported against a physically measured landing point with an uncertainty of about ±0.15 m and is not labeled ATE. The ordering of the tested settings is robust, but this single reference point does not resolve the optimal gate value. The odometry pipeline consumes no satellite positioning: GNSS was used solely by the DJI flight controller for stabilization and waypoint holding. The approach is therefore GPS-denied by construction, but the flight took place at a site where GNSS happened to be available, so this case is addressed by design rather than demonstrated under actual signal deprivation.</p><p><strong>Future work</strong> aims to use the real-time map produced within the Jetson unit to run advanced environmental analysis, turning the drone into a remote-controlled mapping platform capable of onboard AI-driven data processing without satellite positioning. Recent learning-based navigation methods for dynamic environments may provide a direction for integrating autonomous navigation with the proposed UAV mapping system.</p><h2>Acknowledgment</h2><p>This research was funded by The Scientific and Technological Research Council of Türkiye (TÜBİTAK), grant number 123E406, and by the Fırat University Scientific Research Projects Unit (FUBAP), grant numbers MF.24.80 and MF.25.155.</p><h2>References</h2><ol><li>Y. Wu, D. Liu, J. Ma and X. He, "UAV Localization in GPS-Denied Environments: A Review," IEEE Transactions on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics: Systems, doi: 10.1109/TSMC.2026.3708505.</li><li>T. Shan, B. Englot, D. Meyers, W. Wang, C. Ratti and D. Rus, "LIO-SAM: Tightly-coupled Lidar Inertial Odometry via Smoothing and Mapping," 2020 IEEE/RSJ IROS, Las Vegas, NV, USA, 2020, pp. 5135-5142, doi: 10.1109/IROS45743.2020.9341176.</li><li>J. Zhang and S. Singh, "LOAM: Lidar Odometry and Mapping in Real-time," Robotics: Science and Systems, vol. 2, pp. 1-9, 2014, doi: 10.15607/RSS.2014.X.009.</li><li>I. Vizzo, T. Guadagnino, B. Mersch, L. Wiesmann, J. Behley and C. Stachniss, "KISS-ICP: In Defense of Point-to-Point ICP – Simple, Accurate, and Robust Registration If Done the Right Way," IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters, vol. 8, no. 2, pp. 1029-1036, Feb. 2023, doi: 10.1109/LRA.2023.3236571.</li><li>M. V. R. Malladi, T. Guadagnino, L. Lobefaro and C. Stachniss, "Robust Approach for LiDAR-Inertial Odometry Without Sensor-Specific Modeling," IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters, vol. 11, no. 6, pp. 7420-7427, June 2026, doi: 10.1109/LRA.2026.3685966.</li><li>W. Xu, Y. Cai, D. He, J. Lin and F. Zhang, "FAST-LIO2: Fast Direct LiDAR-Inertial Odometry," IEEE Transactions on Robotics, vol. 38, no. 4, pp. 2053-2073, Aug. 2022, doi: 10.1109/TRO.2022.3141876.</li><li>M. El Muhammed, A. M. N. S. Alqadhi, M. A. M. S. Bajhaw and A. Ucar, "MSR-SLAM: A Multi-Stage Map-Refinement Framework for Reliable LiDAR–Inertial SLAM in GPS-Denied Environments," Applied Sciences, 2026.</li><li>A. M. N. S. Alqadhi, M. El Muhammed, M. A. M. S. Bajhaw and A. Ucar, "PV-STAM: Velocity-Aware Attention for Mapless Deep Reinforcement Learning Navigation in Dynamic Environments," Applied Sciences, vol. 16, no. 18, art. no. 9083, 2026, doi: 10.3390/app16189083.</li><li>A. M. N. S. Alqadhi, M. A. M. S. Bajhaw, M. El Muhammed and A. Ucar, "Drift-Resilient Indoor 3D Mapping in GPS-Denied Environments Using RKO-LIO with Velodyne-Pixhawk Integration in ROS 2," 2026 8th International Congress on Human-Computer Interaction, Optimization and Robotic Applications (ICHORA), Ankara, Turkiye, 2026, pp. 1-5, doi: 10.1109/ICHORA69329.2026.11537209.</li><li>S. Macenski et al., "Robot Operating System 2: Design, architecture, and uses in the wild," Science Robotics, vol. 7, no. 66, art. no. eabm6074, 2022, doi: 10.1126/scirobotics.abm6074.</li><li>K. Museth, "VDB: High-Resolution Sparse Volumes with Dynamic Topology," ACM Transactions on Graphics, vol. 32, no. 3, art. no. 27, 2013, doi: 10.1145/2487228.2487235.</li><li>J. Sturm, N. Engelhard, F. Endres, W. Burgard and D. Cremers, "A benchmark for the evaluation of RGB-D SLAM systems," 2012 IEEE/RSJ IROS, Vilamoura-Algarve, Portugal, 2012, pp. 573-580, doi: 10.1109/IROS.2012.6385773.</li></ol>$p$,
